//@ expect: accept
//@ reason: `bytes` is a std::vector<int> parameter whose ENTIRE body is the one
//@ statement `data = bytes;` -- SmartPointerAnalyzer::classifyLastUseFieldAssignedParams
//@ proves this is provably `bytes`'s last use (no statement after it references
//@ `bytes` again, and the body is straight-line: no if/while/for/try/closure
//@ anywhere), so the emitter moves it instead of copying the whole vector.
//@ run: 3
//
// The motivating case (zlib-cling's `pushAll(std::vector<byte> bytes) { input
// = bytes; }`): DanglingChecker's general field-store model
// (vector_field_assignment_still_usable_accepted.clg) correctly keeps `bytes`
// live after a field store IN GENERAL (a linked-list node is routinely reused
// right after being attached to a field) -- but that generality was needlessly
// conservative for the common case where the parameter is never touched again.
// This classification is a STRICTLY NARROWER, additional proof on top of the
// general rule, not a relaxation of it: it only fires when the specific body
// being compiled provably has no later use, proven by a straight-line scan
// (see the analyzer's own doc comment for why anything more complex than
// straight-line control flow gives up rather than trying to analyze it).
// The //@ run: assertion below is the actual behavioral guard -- it would
// still print 3 whether this moves or copies (a move that read stale/emptied
// data instead of the real elements would be a distinct, more severe bug this
// assertion also catches); the sibling test
// vector_field_assignment_still_usable_accepted.clg is what pins "still used
// afterward must not move" specifically, and CLAUDE.md/TODO.md's own commit
// records confirm via inspecting `this->data = std::move(bytes);` in the
// generated C++ that this fixture's underlying shape does move (a compiling,
// correct-output accept fixture cannot on its own distinguish a real move
// from a lucky-because-lucky copy, the same reason
// vector_field_assignment_still_usable_accepted.clg's own reason explains).

class Holder {
public:
    std::vector<int> data;

    void take(std::vector<int> bytes) {
        data = bytes;
    }

    int sum() {
        int total = 0;
        int i = 0;
        while (i < data.size()) {
            total = total + data[i];
            i = i + 1;
        }
        return total;
    }
};

int main() {
    Holder h = new Holder();
    std::vector<int> v;
    v.push_back(1);
    v.push_back(2);
    h.take(v);
    std::cout << h.sum() << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
